<?xml version='1.0' encoding='utf-8'?>
<chapter xml:id="fujita-restrictions">
  <title>Uniform estimates for restriction spaces</title>
  <shorttitle>Uniform restrictions</shorttitle>
  <section>
    <title>Restriction spaces with uniform estimates</title>
    <shorttitle>Restriction estimates</shorttitle>
    <p>Fix a positive integer <m>k</m> for which <m>kL</m> is very ample, and embed <m>X</m> by its complete linear system. For an integral closed subvariety <m>W\subset X</m> of dimension <m>d</m>, define the dimension of the restriction image by<fn>Only the dimension of the image enters the argument. No surjectivity assertion for the restriction map is required.</fn><md number="no">I_W(h)=\dim_{\C}\operatorname{im}\bigl(R_h\longrightarrow H^0(W,hL|_W)\bigr).</md></p>
    <lemma xml:id="lem-uniform-restrictions">
      <statement>
        <p>For every <m>1\le d\le n</m>, there are constants <m>C_d</m> and <m>H_d</m>, depending only on <m>(X,L,k,d)</m>, such that<md number="yes" xml:id="eq-uniform-restrictions">\bigl(d!I_W(h)\bigr)^{1/d}\ge h-C_d
 \qquad(h\ge H_d)</md> for every integral <m>d</m>-dimensional closed subvariety <m>W\subset X</m>.</p>
      </statement>
      <proof>
        <p>Let <m>D_0=k^d</m>. The degree of <m>W</m> in the chosen embedding satisfies<md number="no">D=(kL)^d\cdot W=k^d(L^d\cdot W)\ge D_0,</md> since <m>L^d\cdot W</m> is a positive integer. A general linear projection restricts to a finite surjective morphism <m>\pi:W\longrightarrow\mathbb P^d</m>. One can construct it by successive projections from points outside the current image; each projection is finite by <xref ref="bib-stacks-project" detail="Tag 0B1N"/>. The pullback of <m>\cO_{\mathbb P^d}(1)</m> is <m>kL|_W</m>, so <m>\deg(\pi)=D</m>.</p>
        <p>Let <m>K=\C(\mathbb P^d)</m> and <m>E=\C(W)</m>. The extension <m>E/K</m> has degree <m>D</m> and is separable. Consequently the geometric generic fiber consists of <m>D</m> distinct points. Choose one of the coordinates <m>s_0</m> defining the projection. It is nonzero at the generic point of <m>\mathbb P^d</m>, so all these points belong to the affine chart <m>s_0\ne0</m>.</p>
        <p>Select <m>D_0</m> points in this fiber. Over an algebraic closure <m>\overline K</m>, affine linear functions in the ambient coordinates separate every pair of selected points. For a selected point <m>a</m>, choose, for each other selected point <m>b</m>, a linear function that vanishes at <m>b</m> and is nonzero at <m>a</m>. Their product has degree at most <m>D_0-1</m>, vanishes at all the selected points except <m>a</m>, and is nonzero at <m>a</m>. Homogenizing and multiplying by a power of <m>s_0</m> gives a homogeneous ambient form of degree <m>D_0</m> with the same normalized values. These forms show that evaluation of degree-<m>D_0</m> forms on the selected points has rank <m>D_0</m>.</p>
        <p>Write <m>V_{D_0}</m> for the complex vector space of ambient homogeneous forms of degree <m>D_0</m>. Restriction and division by <m>s_0^{D_0}</m> define a <m>K</m>-linear map<md number="no">V_{D_0}\otimes_{\C}K\longrightarrow E.</md> Its rank is at least <m>D_0</m>, since rank is preserved by extension to <m>\overline K</m> and the preceding evaluation map factors through it. A monomial basis of <m>V_{D_0}</m> spans its image over <m>K</m>. We may therefore select ambient forms <m>F_1,\ldots,F_{D_0}</m> from this basis such that the elements <m>F_i/s_0^{D_0}</m> are linearly independent over <m>K</m>.<fn>The selected forms have complex coefficients, although the rank was checked over <m>\overline K</m>.</fn></p>
        <p>For <m>q\ge D_0</m>, multiply each <m>F_i</m> by every monomial of degree <m>q-D_0</m> in the <m>d+1</m> coordinates of the projection. The resulting sections of <m>qkL|_W</m> are linearly independent. A relation between them, divided by <m>s_0^q</m>, would be a relation over <m>K</m> between the <m>F_i/s_0^{D_0}</m>. Each coefficient would vanish as a rational function on <m>\mathbb P^d</m>, and hence the homogeneous polynomial giving that coefficient would vanish identically. All these products come from <m>R_{qk}</m>. It follows that<md number="yes" xml:id="eq-restriction-binomial">I_W(qk)\ge D_0\binom{q-D_0+d}{d}.</md> Taking <m>d</m>th roots and using <m>D_0^{1/d}=k</m> gives<md number="yes" xml:id="eq-restriction-multiples">\bigl(d!I_W(qk)\bigr)^{1/d}
 \ge k(q-D_0)=qk-k^{d+1}.</md></p>
        <p>For every residue class <m>r</m> modulo <m>k</m>, choose once and for all an integer <m>h_r</m> in that class such that <m>h_rL</m> is globally generated. For any given integral <m>W</m>, there is a section of <m>h_rL</m> whose restriction to <m>W</m> is nonzero. Multiplication by its restriction injects the image of <m>R_{qk}</m> on <m>W</m> into the image of <m>R_{qk+h_r}</m>, since a product of two nonzero sections on an integral variety is nonzero. If <m>h=qk+h_r</m> and <m>q\ge D_0</m>, then <xref ref="eq-restriction-multiples" text="global"/> gives<md number="no">\bigl(d!I_W(h)\bigr)^{1/d}\ge h-(h_r+k^{d+1}).</md> We may take<md number="no">C_d=H_d=\max_r h_r+k^{d+1}.</md> These choices work for every <m>W</m>, independently of its degree and singularities.</p>
      </proof>
    </lemma>
  </section>
</chapter>
